<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">A score of 99.901% accuracy can hide a flood of false alarms.</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base"><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>1,000,000 cases</strong><p>Only 10 are truly positive.</p></div><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>1,000 alarms</strong><p>10 real and 990 false.</p></div></div>
<svg viewBox="0 0 470 140" class="w-full" role="img" aria-labelledby="accuracy-title accuracy-desc"><title id="accuracy-title">Nearly all predictions correct but only one percent of alarms correct</title><desc id="accuracy-desc">One thousand flagged alarms include ten true cases and nine hundred ninety false alarms.</desc>
<rect x="16" y="40" width="438" height="34" fill="#bd4b37" fill-opacity=".55"/>
<rect x="16" y="40" width="4.38" height="34" fill="#387a91"/>
<g fill="currentColor" font-size="17"><text x="16" y="25">Among the 1,000 alarms</text><text x="16" y="106">10 true</text><text x="300" y="106">990 false</text><text x="16" y="132" font-weight="bold">Precision = 1%, despite 99.901% overall accuracy</text></g></svg>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The example catches all 10 rare cases (100% recall) and correctly labels 999,010 of 1,000,000 cases (99.901% accuracy). But only 10 of the 1,000 alarms are real. Counting all easy negatives makes accuracy look spectacular while alarms remain unreliable.</figcaption></figure>